<h2 id="chips">The One Line in That Table That Matters</h2>

<p>Look at the chip row. The SF-2000 names the bin: Samsung LM301B. The P2000 says "Samsung LED" without naming the bin.</p>

<p>An LED bin is a grade within a production run. Two diodes can be the same model and different bins, and the higher bin converts the same watt into more light. That is the whole reason two boards can draw 200W and not perform the same.</p>

<p>Does that make the P2000 a bad light? No. It makes the SF-2000 the one where you know what you are buying. When one brand publishes the bin and the other does not, the honest read is that the published number is worth paying a little more for.</p>

<p>The rating row agrees with that reading, mildly. 4.7 across 4,100 reviews is a stronger signal than 4.5 across 2,800, and the review counts are close enough that both are meaningful samples.</p>

<h2 id="coverage">Where the Cheaper One Actually Wins</h2>

<p>Coverage claim. The P2000 states 3.5x3.5 feet in veg against the SF-2000's flat 3x3. If you are running a 3x3 tent full of leafy greens and you want the widest spread for the least money, the 3.5 foot claim is the number you are buying. The SF-2000 will not match it.</p>

<p>Then be careful with the flower number. Both drop to roughly 2.5x2.5 to 3x3 when you flip to flowering, which is normal. A 200W board does not hold a 3.5 foot square in flower no matter what the listing says. If you want to flower a 4x4 area you need 300W or more, and you are shopping in the <a href="/grow-lights">full grow light database</a> at that point, not between these two.</p>

<h2 id="pick">Which One Should You Buy</h2>

<p><strong>Buy the SF-2000 ($145.99)</strong> if you want the named chip bin, the higher rating, and a straight 3x3 footprint for a tent that size. This is the one I would pick for a mixed vegetable grow.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Spider+Farmer+SF-2000+LED+grow+light&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check the SF-2000 price on Amazon</a></p>

<p><strong>Buy the P2000 ($131.99)</strong> if you want the widest veg coverage for the least money, and you are growing greens rather than fruiting plants.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Viparspectra+P2000+LED+grow+light&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check the P2000 price on Amazon</a></p>

<p>Either way, buy a dimmable board. Both of these are, and you will use dimming more than you expect. Seedlings want a fraction of full power, and a light you cannot turn down is a light that burns your first crop. If you want to see the whole field at this wattage including the Migro Aray and the HLG options, the <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">grow light comparison</a> has the specs side by side.</p>

<p>Before you buy, work out two things. First, how many hours your crop actually wants, covered in <a href="/posts/how-much-light-does-lettuce-need">how much light lettuce needs per day</a>. Second, how you will feed the plants sitting under the light, which <a href="/posts/dwc-vs-kratky">DWC vs Kratky</a> lays out with real part costs for both builds.</p>

